#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
ghworkflowhunt - DFIR triage for malicious GitHub Actions workflows.

For each repo (owner/name):
  1. Mirror-clones it (all branches, tags, and refs/pull/*) into an evidence dir,
     with gc disabled so nothing gets pruned.
  2. (API) Pulls every workflow run created on/after --since, saves the raw JSON,
     fetches the exact workflow YAML that ran (by head_sha, so it works even if the
     file or branch was deleted afterward), and flags runs matching your patterns.
     For flagged runs it downloads logs, job details, and artifacts, and fetches
     the run's head commit into the mirror under refs/dfir/runs/<run_id>.
  3. (Git) Walks every commit in every ref that touched .github/, and records which
     patterns appear on added (+) or removed (-) lines. Removed-line hits catch
     "add workflow, let it run, delete it" cleanup.
  4. Flags ref names (branches/tags) matching the strong patterns.
  5. Writes a SHA-256 manifest of everything collected.

  Patterns come in two tiers:
    strong      attack-specific (mkdir out, toJSON(secrets)); one hit flags.
                Every strong-flagged run gets full evidence collection.
    weak        common in normal workflows (curl, wget, base64, upload-artifact,
                pull_request_target). Always recorded in the TSVs for context, but
                they flag only when --weak-threshold distinct ones (default 2)
                appear together. A weak-combo workflow version is collected
                once; later runs of the same version get run.json only and point
                to the collected run in the evidence_run_id column.

  At the end it prints a formatted report to stdout and saves a plain-text copy
  to <out>/summary.txt (latest) and <out>/summary_<timestamp>.txt. The report
  separates attacker workflows that actually executed from ones that never ran
  (held for approval, skipped, or failed to start; those never produce logs or
  artifacts), and lists "Evidence gaps": runs that executed but whose logs or
  expected artifacts are missing.

  For each attacker run that executed, it reads the run's own logs to see what
  the upload step did (artifact ID, size, SHA-256, or "No files were found"),
  then looks up each logged artifact ID in the API. An ID that existed and now
  returns 404 before its retention ran out is reported as DELETED. With
  --audit-log (org owner token), it also pulls artifact.destroy and
  checks.delete_logs events from the org audit log and matches them by ID.

  Tables are tab-separated (.tsv). Color is used only when stderr is a
  terminal; set NO_COLOR=1 or pass --no-color to turn it off.

Usage:
  Set GITHUB_TOKEN near the top of this file (or export it in your shell),
  using a token from a CLEAN account; read-only public access is enough.
  The token is checked against GitHub before any work starts.

  python3 ghworkflowhunt owner/repo1 owner/repo2 --since 2026-10-23 --out evidence

  Add patterns (strong / weak), or change how many weak hits it takes to flag:
  python3 ghworkflowhunt owner/repo --pattern 'exfil' --weak-pattern 'webhook\\.site'
  python3 ghworkflowhunt owner/repo --weak-threshold 3
  python3 ghworkflowhunt owner/repo --weak-threshold 0     # strong only

  Replace all built-in patterns:
  python3 ghworkflowhunt owner/repo --only-patterns --pattern 'foo_' --weak-pattern 'curl'

Requires: Python 3.8+, git on PATH. No third-party packages.
"""
